      "observed": "On the Power sheet the 4-pin fan headers are wired with +12V on pin 1, the tach signal (FAN_0_TACH / FAN_1_TACH) on pin 2, and GND on pin 3 (pin 4 no-connect).",
      "expected": "The specification states the fan-header 12 V pins are pin 2 ('the two fan-header 12 V pins (J7/J8 pin 2)'), i.e. the +12V rail should land on pin 2, not pin 1. The drawing therefore places +12V, the tach line and GND on the wrong connector pins relative to the intended design.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "Power sheet (power.kicad_sch): J7 / J8 (Conn_01x04_Pin) — pin 1 -> +12V, pin 2 -> FAN_0_TACH / FAN_1_TACH, pin 3 -> GND, pin 4 -> NC"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "the two fan-header 12 V pins (J7/J8 pin 2)"}
      ],
      "impact": "A standard 4-pin fan plugged into J7/J8 would receive +12V on its GND pin and GND on its +12V pin, with the tach pin driven at +12V; the fan will not spin correctly, the tach/stall-detection input will see the wrong level (and may be damaged), and fan monitoring will not behave as intended.",
      "confidence": 0.6
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The board is otherwise a faithful match to the specification (all values, the power-mux, buck, heater high-side switch, SPI thermocouple channels and USB-C CC pull-downs check out), but the fan-header pinout is miswired: the drawing puts +12V on J7/J8 pin 1 whereas the spec defines the 12V pin as pin 2, swapping the supply, tach and ground lines on the connector."
}

